    "description": "If the zoned premium beds above are more than you want to spend, the Signature Hybrid is where we would go next. Its strength is choice. Brooklyn Bedding sells it in Soft, Medium and Firm, and says plainly that Soft is for side sleepers, Medium for combination sleepers and Firm for back and stomach sleepers. For most people with back pain that makes the Medium or the Firm the right version, and the Amazon listings carry both. The core is 8 inches of Ascension X pocketed coils, up to 760 in the largest size according to the maker, under roughly 3.5 inches of comfort foam. That is a thick, sturdy coil unit for the price, and it is what keeps heavier hips from sinking. The maker lists a 365-night trial and a limited lifetime warranty, which is generous at this level. What you give up is zoning: the coils are uniform from edge to edge, so the mattress cannot be firmer under your lower back than under your shoulders. For back and stomach sleepers that matters less, because their weight is spread more evenly. Side sleepers with back pain will usually do better on the Helix Midnight Luxe. Note the return rules too: the trial is one-time, and the maker asks for 30 days on the bed before you start a return."
  },
  {
    "id": "dreamcloud-classic-hybrid",
    "name": "DreamCloud Classic 12\" Hybrid Mattress",
    "category": "12-Inch Hybrid, Gel Memory Foam, Pocketed Coils, Medium Firm",
    "badge": "Best Mid-Price Medium-Firm",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Listed as medium firm, the feel most people with back pain start with",
      "Gel memory foam over pocketed coils, with a cashmere-blend cover",
      "Coils add bounce and airflow compared with all-foam beds",
      "365-night trial named in the Amazon listing",
      "Widely discounted on Amazon during sales"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Reviewers disagree on firmness, from 6 to 8 out of 10",
      "No zoned support",
      "Heavier sleepers may want the taller Premier or Luxe"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A solid medium-firm hybrid at a middle price for back sleepers who do not need zoning.",
    "description": "The DreamCloud Classic is the mattress to look at if you want a medium-firm hybrid from a large brand without paying for a premium build. DreamCloud reworked its lineup in 2025, and the Classic is now a 12-inch hybrid: gel memory foam comfort layers over individually pocketed coils, under a cashmere-blend cover. The Amazon listing calls it medium firm. Independent reviewers do not quite agree on how firm, with ratings from about 6 to 8 out of 10, but all of them put it on the supportive side of medium, which is where most back sleepers with lower back pain land. The memory foam gives a little contouring at the hips so the lumbar curve is filled rather than left hanging, and the coils underneath keep it from feeling stuck. It has no zoned support, so it relies on overall firmness rather than a firmer middle. That suits back sleepers well and side sleepers less well. The listing names a 365-night trial, which gives you a full season to judge whether your back feels better. If you are a heavier sleeper, or you want a thicker comfort layer, DreamCloud's Premier and Luxe models sit above it; if you want zoning, the Helix beds are the better choice at a higher price."
  },
  {
    "id": "nectar-classic",
    "name": "Nectar Classic 12\" Memory Foam Mattress",
    "category": "12-Inch All-Foam, Medium Firm, Cooling Cover",
    "badge": "Best Memory Foam",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Contouring memory foam fills the gap under the lower back",
      "Listed as medium firm on most sizes",
      "Very little motion transfer for couples",
      "365-night trial and Forever Warranty listed by Nectar",
      "Fiberglass-free with CertiPUR-US certified foams"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slower to change position on than a hybrid",
      "Some listings label one size firm, so check the size you buy",
      "Can feel warm to people who sleep hot"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The memory foam pick for back pain if you like a hugging feel and share the bed with a restless partner.",
    "description": "Memory foam suits a lot of people with back pain because of how it fills space. Lie on your back on a firm innerspring and there is often a small gap under the lower back; memory foam softens under the hips and rises into that gap, so the lumbar curve is supported along its length. The Nectar Classic is one of the best-known all-foam beds sold on Amazon and is built around that idea. It is 12 inches tall, with memory foam comfort layers over a thick foam base and a cooling cover, and Nectar lists it as medium firm. Nectar markets it for back pain relief; we would put it more modestly and say that many back sleepers who like a hugging feel do well on it. It also isolates motion very well, which matters if a partner's tossing is part of why you sleep badly. Nectar lists a 365-night trial and its Forever Warranty, and the foams are CertiPUR-US certified and fiberglass-free. The trade-offs are the usual memory foam ones. It is slower to move on than a hybrid, which some people with stiff backs find annoying when turning over, and it can feel warm. One Amazon size listing calls it firm while others say medium firm, so check the label for the size you order. If you change position a lot, choose a hybrid."
  },
  {
    "id": "siena-signature-10",
    "name": "Siena Signature 10\" Memory Foam Mattress",
    "category": "10-Inch Memory Foam, Medium Firm, 180-Night Trial",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Brand-rated 6.5 out of 10, a medium-firm feel",
      "Low price for a named brand with a real trial",
      "180-night trial and 10-year warranty listed",
      "CertiPUR-US certified and fiberglass-free foams",
      "Some reviewers find it firmer than rated, which suits back sleepers"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Thinner comfort layers than the beds above",
      "Edge support is limited, as with most 10-inch foam beds",
      "Not ideal for sleepers over about 230 pounds"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The budget pick for back pain: a firm-leaning medium foam bed that will not undo your back or your bank account.",
    "description": "Not everyone with a sore back can spend a thousand dollars on a mattress, and the good news is that you do not have to. The Siena Signature is a 10-inch, three-layer memory foam bed that Siena rates at 6.5 out of 10, firmly in medium-firm territory. Independent reviewers disagree on how it feels: some put it near 6, and one panel rated it much firmer. For a back sleeper with lower back pain, landing on the firm side of medium is not a bad thing, because the common cause of morning stiffness on cheap mattresses is sagging rather than firmness. Siena lists a 180-night trial and a 10-year warranty, and the foams are CertiPUR-US certified and fiberglass-free. The trade-offs are what you would expect at the price. The comfort layers are thinner than on the premium beds, so side sleepers with sharp shoulders may want a topper on it, and edge support is limited, as on most 10-inch foam mattresses. Sleepers over about 230 pounds should spend more on a coil bed with a deeper support core. For a guest room, a first apartment, or a back sleeper who wants to see whether medium-firm helps before spending more, it is the most sensible place to start."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Mattress for Back Pain",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-mattress-for-back-pain/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best mattress for back pain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, the best mattress for back pain is a medium-firm hybrid with zoned support, such as the Nolah Evolution 15 in Luxury Firm. It lets the shoulders and hips sink slightly while holding the waist up. Stomach sleepers and heavier back sleepers often prefer something firmer, like the Helix Dawn Luxe, and budget shoppers can start with the Siena Signature."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a firm or medium-firm mattress better for lower back pain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Medium-firm suits most people with lower back pain better than very firm. Research comparing firmness levels has generally favoured medium-firm beds. A very firm surface can leave a gap under the lower back and press on the shoulders and hips. Firm beds make more sense for stomach sleepers and people over about 230 pounds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a mattress cause back pain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A mattress can contribute to back pain, especially if it sags or is badly matched to your weight and position. Waking stiff and loosening up within an hour is a common sign. A new mattress may help in that situation, but persistent or worsening pain is worth discussing with a doctor or physical therapist."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is memory foam good for back pain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Memory foam can be good for back pain because it contours to the body and fills the gap under the lower back. Many back sleepers and couples like it. Its downsides are a slower feel when changing position and more heat retention. People who move a lot or weigh over 230 pounds often prefer a hybrid."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is zoned support in a mattress?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Zoned support means the mattress is firmer in some areas than others, usually firmer under the hips and lower back and softer under the shoulders. Makers do this with different coils or cut foam. It helps keep the spine level because the hips are heavier than the shoulders, and it suits side sleepers especially well."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should it take to adjust to a new mattress?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most people need two to four weeks to adjust to a new mattress, and some feel slightly worse in the first week before improving. That is why many makers ask you to wait about 30 days before returning. Keep a short morning log of how your back feels so you judge the trend, not one bad night."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a mattress topper help my back pain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A topper may help if your mattress is still flat and supportive but feels too firm, because it adds pressure relief at the shoulders and hips. It will not fix a mattress that sags, and it cannot make a soft bed firm. If there is a visible dip where you sleep, replacing the mattress is the better option."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Bedroom & Sleep",
    tags: [
      "best mattress for back pain",
      "best mattress for lower back pain",
      "best mattress for bad back",
      "what is the best mattress for back pain",
      "best mattress for back pain relief",
      "zoned support mattress"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Mattress for <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Back Pain</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Hard is not the answer. For most people with a sore lower back, the right bed is medium-firm, zoned under the waist and matched to how they sleep.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#nolah-evolution-15"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Rankings
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#firm-vs-medium-firm" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Firm vs Medium-Firm</a>
          <a href="#zoned-support" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Zoned Support Explained</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 7 Best, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#sleep-position" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sleep Position and Alignment</a>
          <a href="#hybrid-vs-foam" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Hybrid or Memory Foam</a>
          <a href="#topper-enough" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. When a Topper Is Enough</a>
          <a href="#sleep-trial" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Using the Sleep Trial</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best mattress for back pain is rarely the firmest one. Most people with lower back pain sleep better on a medium-firm bed that lets the shoulders and hips sink slightly while holding the waist up, and zoned support makes that easier. Here is how to choose, and the seven beds we would shortlist.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="firm-vs-medium-firm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Firm vs Medium-Firm for Back Pain: Medium-Firm Wins for Most People
          </h2>

          <p>
            The oldest advice about back pain and mattresses is to sleep on something hard. It is also the advice most likely to be wrong. When researchers have compared firmness levels for people with chronic lower back pain, medium-firm beds have tended to come out ahead of very firm ones. The reason is simple once you picture a spine lying on a surface. A healthy spine is not straight; it has a gentle curve at the neck and another at the lower back. A mattress that keeps you comfortable has to let the heavy parts of the body sink a little while holding up the lighter parts, so that the curve stays natural instead of being flattened or exaggerated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Too Firm Backfires</h3>

          <p>
            On a very firm mattress the shoulders and hips are pushed up, and the waist is left unsupported. Back sleepers often feel this as a gap under the lower back, and side sleepers feel it as sore shoulders and a spine that bends upward in the middle. The body responds by tensing muscles to hold position, which is the opposite of what a sore back needs overnight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Too Soft Backfires</h3>

          <p>
            A mattress that is too soft, or one that has sagged with age, lets the hips sink below the shoulders. The lower back then sits in a hammock shape for seven or eight hours. This is the most common reason people wake up stiff and loosen up by mid-morning, and it is why an old, dipped mattress is so often the real culprit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Firm Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            Firm mattresses still have a place. Stomach sleepers usually need one, because a soft bed lets the hips drop and arches the lower back. Back sleepers over about 230 pounds often need one too, because their weight compresses a medium bed further. For everyone else, start at medium-firm, roughly a 6 to 7 out of 10, and adjust from there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Firmness Is Relative to Body Weight</h3>

          <p>
            Firmness ratings assume an average adult of around 150 to 200 pounds. A 130-pound sleeper experiences a 7 as firm; a 250-pound sleeper experiences the same bed as medium. Read every rating with your own weight in mind, and if you are well above average, see our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-heavy-people/">best mattresses for heavy people</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="zoned-support" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Zoned Support: The Feature That Matters Most for a Bad Back
          </h2>

          <p>
            Zoned support means the mattress is not equally firm along its length. The section under your hips and lower back is made firmer, and the section under your shoulders is made softer. The point is to deal with the fact that the body is not evenly heavy. The hips are the heaviest part of most people, so on a uniform mattress they sink furthest, and the spine tilts with them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Zoning Works</h3>

          <p>
            Most makers zone the coil unit. They use thicker or more tightly wound coils in the middle third of the bed and softer coils toward the head end. Others zone a foam layer by cutting it into sections of different density. Either approach aims for the same result: a little more push-back at the waist, a little more give at the shoulder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Zoned Coils vs Zoned Foam</h3>

          <p>
            Zoned coils tend to have a clearer effect, because the coil unit carries most of your weight. Zoned foam is subtler and usually sits higher up, where it shapes comfort more than support. Helix and Nolah both describe zoned coils on the models below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who Benefits Most</h3>

          <p>
            Side sleepers with lower back pain gain the most from zoning, because they need a soft shoulder and a supported waist at the same time. Back sleepers benefit too. Stomach sleepers benefit less; they mainly need overall firmness. If you are on a budget, a well-made medium-firm bed without zoning is still better than a cheap zoned one that sags.
          </p>

