        <div id="sleep-position" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sleep Position and Spine Alignment: Match the Bed to How You Sleep
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most useful thing to know before buying is which position you actually sleep in, not the one you fall asleep in. The goal in every position is the same: a spine that keeps roughly the shape it has when you stand up straight. What changes is which part of the mattress has to do the work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Back sleepers need the lumbar curve filled. A medium-firm mattress with a few inches of contouring foam usually does this well. Many people also find a small pillow under the knees takes pressure off the lower back. Zoning helps, but it is not essential.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pillow height matters more than most back sleepers expect. A thick pillow tips the head forward and pulls on the upper back, while a pillow that is too thin lets the neck drop. Aim for one that keeps your face roughly level with the ceiling. Back sleepers between about 130 and 230 pounds usually do well on a 6 to 7 out of 10; lighter people often prefer the softer end of that range, and heavier people the firmer end.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Side sleepers need the shoulder and hip to sink so the spine stays level from neck to tailbone. That calls for a softer surface, around 5 to 6 out of 10, over firm support. A pillow between the knees keeps the top leg from twisting the pelvis. Our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-side-sleepers/">best mattress for side sleepers</a> goes deeper.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stomach Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Stomach sleeping is the hardest position on the lower back, because the hips naturally sink and arch the spine. Stomach sleepers usually do best on a firm mattress, 7 to 8 out of 10, with a thin pillow or none at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combination Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            If you move between positions, pick medium-firm and favour a hybrid. Coils make turning over easier, and a bed that is not extreme in either direction works reasonably in every position.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hybrid-vs-foam" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Hybrid or Memory Foam for Lower Back Pain?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both types can work for a bad back, and neither is automatically better. The choice comes down to how you move at night, how warm you sleep, and how much you weigh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid Mattresses</h3>

          <p>
            A hybrid puts a few inches of foam over a pocketed coil core. The coils give firm, durable support and some bounce, which makes it easier to change position, and they let air move through the mattress. Hybrids are usually the better choice for heavier sleepers and for anyone who turns over often. All the zoned beds on this list are hybrids.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Memory Foam Mattresses</h3>

          <p>
            All-foam beds contour closely and isolate motion very well. They are often cheaper, too. The downsides are a slower, sinking feel that some people with stiff backs find hard to move in, and more heat retention. Lighter sleepers and couples often prefer them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            If you weigh over 230 pounds, sleep hot, or move a lot, buy a hybrid. If you sleep still, share the bed with a restless partner and like a hugging feel, memory foam is a reasonable choice. For a broader look at the whole category, see our main guide to the <a href="/best-mattress/">best mattress</a> for every sleeper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="topper-enough" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When a Mattress Topper Is Enough (and When It Is Not)
          </h2>

          <p>
            A new mattress is a big purchase, and sometimes it is not the right one. A topper can change the feel of a bed for a fraction of the cost. The question is whether your problem is the feel of the surface or the support underneath it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Topper Can Fix It</h3>

          <p>
            If your mattress is less than about seven years old, is flat when you lie a straight edge across it, and simply feels too firm, a 2 to 3 inch memory foam or latex topper can add the pressure relief your shoulders and hips are missing. This is common for side sleepers who bought a firm bed hoping it would help their back. Our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-topper/">best mattress topper</a> covers the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Topper Cannot Fix It</h3>

          <p>
            A topper cannot fix sagging. If there is a visible dip where you sleep, a soft topper will simply follow the dip, and your hips will still sink. A topper also cannot make a soft bed firmer in any meaningful way. In both cases the support core is the problem, and only a new mattress solves it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Floor Test</h3>

          <p>
            If you are unsure, try sleeping on the floor, or on a firm guest bed, for two or three nights. If your back feels clearly better, your mattress has probably lost its support. If it feels worse, the issue may be pressure, and a topper is worth trying first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sleep-trial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Use a Sleep Trial When You Have Back Pain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every mattress on this list comes with a home trial, and that trial is the most useful tool you have. Firmness ratings, reviews and guides like this one can narrow the choice, but only a few weeks on the bed tell you whether it helps your back.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Give It at Least 30 Nights</h3>

          <p>
            Your body needs time to adjust to a new surface, especially if the old one sagged. Most makers ask you to wait about 30 days before returning, and that is a sensible minimum anyway. Some people feel a little worse in the first week before feeling better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep a Simple Log</h3>

          <p>
            Write down how your back feels each morning on a scale of one to ten, and note the position you woke in. After a month the pattern is usually clear. It also gives you an honest answer instead of a vague impression.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Know the Return Rules Before You Buy</h3>

          <p>
            Check whether the trial applies when you buy through Amazon, whether there is a return fee, and whether the trial can be used only once. If you are comparing costs and timing your purchase around a sale, our <a href="/mattress-prices-and-deals/">mattress prices and deals</a> guide explains what is normal. Talk to your doctor or physical therapist about persistent pain; a mattress may help, but it is not a treatment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Firmest Bed in the Store</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Very firm mattresses push the shoulders and hips up and leave the lower back unsupported. Unless you sleep on your stomach or weigh well over 230 pounds, start at medium-firm and only go firmer if the trial tells you to.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the Firmness When the Bed Is Sagging</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An old mattress with a dip in the middle will hurt your back whatever its original firmness. Lay a broom handle across it. If you can see a gap of more than an inch, the support has gone, and a topper will not bring it back.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Your Sleep Position</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bed that is perfect for a back sleeper can be painful for a side sleeper. Choose the firmness for the position you wake up in, not the one you fall asleep in, and choose zoning if you sleep on your side.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Returning the Mattress After a Week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Your body needs time to adjust, particularly after years on a sagging bed. Give a new mattress at least 30 nights, keep a simple morning log, and decide on the pattern rather than the first few days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Good Mattress on a Bad Base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sagging box spring or a slatted frame with wide gaps will undo the support of any mattress. Check that the base is flat, solid and suited to the mattress type before blaming the new bed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

