        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress for back pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the best mattress for back pain is a medium-firm hybrid with zoned support, such as the Nolah Evolution 15 in Luxury Firm. It lets the shoulders and hips sink slightly while holding the waist up. Stomach sleepers and heavier back sleepers often prefer something firmer, like the Helix Dawn Luxe, and budget shoppers can start with the Siena Signature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a firm or medium-firm mattress better for lower back pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Medium-firm suits most people with lower back pain better than very firm. Research comparing firmness levels has generally favoured medium-firm beds. A very firm surface can leave a gap under the lower back and press on the shoulders and hips. Firm beds make more sense for stomach sleepers and people over about 230 pounds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a mattress cause back pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A mattress can contribute to back pain, especially if it sags or is badly matched to your weight and position. Waking stiff and loosening up within an hour is a common sign. A new mattress may help in that situation, but persistent or worsening pain is worth discussing with a doctor or physical therapist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is memory foam good for back pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Memory foam can be good for back pain because it contours to the body and fills the gap under the lower back. Many back sleepers and couples like it. Its downsides are a slower feel when changing position and more heat retention. People who move a lot or weigh over 230 pounds often prefer a hybrid.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is zoned support in a mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Zoned support means the mattress is firmer in some areas than others, usually firmer under the hips and lower back and softer under the shoulders. Makers do this with different coils or cut foam. It helps keep the spine level because the hips are heavier than the shoulders, and it suits side sleepers especially well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should it take to adjust to a new mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most people need two to four weeks to adjust to a new mattress, and some feel slightly worse in the first week before improving. That is why many makers ask you to wait about 30 days before returning. Keep a short morning log of how your back feels so you judge the trend, not one bad night.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a mattress topper help my back pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A topper may help if your mattress is still flat and supportive but feels too firm, because it adds pressure relief at the shoulders and hips. It will not fix a mattress that sags, and it cannot make a soft bed firm. If there is a visible dip where you sleep, replacing the mattress is the better option.
              </p>
